  "question": {
    "text": "A wire of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math>&nbsp;and radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;is clamped rigidly at one end. When the other end of the wire is pulled by a force<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>f</mi></math>, its length increases by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi></math>. Another wire of same material of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></math>&nbsp;and radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;is pulled by a force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>f</mi></math>. Then the increase in its length will be:",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "s3ClD",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>l</mi></math>",
